# =============================================================================
# Poker Risk Lab — example environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and edit. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so it must
# never contain a real secret, a real host or a real credential.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every variable in the API sections below is read once, at startup, by
# apps/api/src/config.ts. That file validates all of them together and refuses to
# boot with a partial config, so a typo here becomes one clear error at boot rather
# than a mystery at 3am.
#
# The last three sections are read by something OTHER than the API server — the web
# app, the migration runner, the scripts. They are listed here because "every
# variable in the repo has one place to look it up" is the only property that makes
# this file trustworthy, and a variable that decides where the browser sends every
# request going undocumented is exactly how a deployment ends up pointing at
# localhost.
#
# THIS IS AN EDUCATIONAL PLAY-MONEY APP. There is no payment provider, no wallet
# and no cash-out, so there is nothing here to configure for any of those.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# development | test | production. Controls every strict-versus-friendly choice
# in config.ts (secret strength, HSTS, cookie prefixes, logger).
NODE_ENV=development

# Port the API listens on.
# 3001 in local development, because that is what the web client defaults to
# (apps/web/src/lib/api.ts), what docker-compose.yml publishes, and what the README and
# runbook document. These disagreeing once meant the API listened on 8080 while the
# browser called 3001 — every request failed and nothing said why.
PORT=3001

# Interface to bind. 127.0.0.1 by default so a development server is not exposed
# to the local network. In a container set 0.0.0.0 — but only when something in
# front of it controls who can reach the port.
HOST=127.0.0.1

# fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace | silent.
# Defaults to info, or silent under NODE_ENV=test.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser origins
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Comma-separated exact allowlist, used for BOTH the CORS check and the
# WebSocket Origin check — one list, so the two can never disagree.
#
# Each entry must be a bare origin: scheme, host, optional port. No trailing
# slash, no path, no wildcard. `https://app.example` is fine;
# `https://app.example/` and `https://*.example` are rejected at boot.
#
# In production every entry must be https, because a Secure session cookie is
# never sent to an http origin and sign-in could not work.
#
# Both loopback spellings are listed below because `localhost` and `127.0.0.1` are
# DIFFERENT ORIGINS to a browser, and the dev loop reaches the app by either. An
# allowlist naming only one produces a failure that reads like a bug in the app rather
# than a bug in this file: the page renders, every API call is refused by CORS, and the
# client degrades as though the API were undeployed. `next.config.ts` allows both for
# the same reason.
PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000,http://127.0.0.1:3000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session cookie signing key  (REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION — GENERATE YOUR OWN)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Generate one with exactly this command and paste the output:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Rules enforced at boot in production: at least 32 characters, at least 12
# distinct characters, and it must not read like a placeholder. The value below
# is deliberately one that config.ts REFUSES in production — if you forget to
# replace it, the server will not start, which is the point.
#
# Outside production a missing value is replaced by a random per-process key.
# That is safe but it invalidates every existing session on each restart, so set
# one locally if you are tired of signing in again.
#
# Rotating this invalidates all sessions. That is the intended way to log
# everybody out.
SESSION_COOKIE_SECRET=replace-me-this-example-value-is-rejected-in-production

# Whether cookies get the Secure attribute. Defaults to true in production and
# false elsewhere. Setting it to false in production is refused at boot.
# Only set this at all if you are doing something unusual behind a proxy.
#COOKIE_SECURE=false

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# postgres:// or postgresql://. Local development matches `pnpm run db:up`.
# Never commit a real one: this string contains a password.
# The password MUST match docker-compose.yml, which defaults POSTGRES_PASSWORD to
# "poker-dev-only". These two files disagreeing is a real trap: following the runbook
# verbatim produced a 28P01 invalid_password against a healthy container, and nothing in
# the error suggested the docs were at fault rather than the setup. If you override
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD for compose, change it here too — compose substitutes its own default,
# while these host-side db:* scripts read this line.
#
# TWO identities, because the API and the migration job have different blast radii.
# This is the shape docs/RUNBOOK.md §5 describes for a deployment, and development now
# runs it rather than only reading about it.
#
#   DATABASE_URL            the API, the test suite and `pnpm run db:verify`. Connects as
#                           prl_api, whose only privileges come from membership in
#                           prl_app: DML on the app tables, no CREATE, no TRUNCATE, no
#                           ownership, and nothing at all on prl.schema_migrations.
#   DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL  migrations, seeds and role creation. apps/api/scripts/*.mjs
#                           read it in preference to DATABASE_URL; anything that only
#                           ever set DATABASE_URL keeps working unchanged.
#
# Why it matters that these differ locally: until they did, every development query ran
# as the cluster superuser (rolsuper, rolbypassrls), so a query needing a grant prl_app
# does not hold could not fail here. It failed for the first time in production, as a
# permission-denied at request time. Now it fails on your machine, which is the point.
#
# The migration line stays the cluster owner locally, and that is deliberate rather than
# lazy. It is the identity that CREATES the two login roles below (via
# db/seeds/0002_dev_login_roles.sql, applied by `pnpm run db:seed`), so it has to work on
# a cluster where neither of them exists yet — including the very first `pnpm run
# db:setup`, and including the day you need `db:seed` to reset a forgotten prl_api
# password. A deployment names prl_deployer here instead, out of a secret manager
# (docs/RUNBOOK.md §5); the seed creates that role locally too, so
# `DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL=postgresql://prl_deployer:poker-dev-only@...` rehearses the
# deployment identity whenever you want to check a migration under it.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://prl_api:poker-dev-only@127.0.0.1:5432/poker_risk_lab
DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL=postgresql://poker:poker-dev-only@127.0.0.1:5432/poker_risk_lab

# Maximum server-side connections in the pool. Keep the total across all
# processes below Postgres' own max_connections.
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Per-statement ceiling, in milliseconds, applied to every connection. A query
# that exceeds it is cancelled by Postgres. Postgres' own default is unlimited,
# which is how one bad query becomes an outage.
DATABASE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Seconds an idle pooled connection is kept before being closed.
DATABASE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# Seconds to wait for a new connection before giving up.
DATABASE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional sign-in with an external account (OIDC)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# All four or none. Three out of four is refused at boot, because that is the
# state where the sign-in button appears and then fails in a way that looks like
# the identity provider's fault.
#
# Leave all four commented out to run with guest sessions only; the API then
# answers `oidc_not_configured` honestly and the web app hides the button.
#
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET is a real credential. Do not commit it.
#OIDC_ISSUER=https://accounts.example.test
#OIDC_CLIENT_ID=poker-risk-lab-local
#OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=replace-me
#OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/oidc/callback

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Computation limits
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Hard ceiling on Monte Carlo samples per request, whatever a client asks for.
# Keep this at or below the 500000 maximum in the shared equity schema so a
# request the schema accepts is never silently clamped to something smaller.
MONTE_CARLO_MAX_SAMPLES=200000

# Worker threads for equity work. Defaults to min(4, cores - 1): equity is
# CPU-bound, and a pool larger than the machine turns one expensive request into
# latency for every other request.
#MONTE_CARLO_WORKERS=3

# Wall-clock budget for one Coach analysis, in milliseconds. Past this the
# request answers `computation_timeout` rather than holding a connection.
# How long a lone learner gets to act, in seconds, when every other seat at the table
# is a bot. The default is deliberately long: the Coach panel a learner is meant to
# read at that moment runs to roughly 950 words, and the previous 60-second clock
# auto-folded readers mid-analysis — the product's two headline features in direct
# conflict. Nothing is at stake at a play-money table against bots, so reading time
# costs nothing. A room with a second human in it keeps its own shorter clock, because
# there one player's reading time is everybody else's waiting time.
#
# Range 5-900. The timeout itself never goes away: an abandoned seat still folds.
LEARNER_TURN_SECONDS=240

COACH_TIMEOUT_MS=3000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limiting
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Keyed on the session when there is one, and on a keyed hash of the client
# address otherwise. Never on a raw IP — see plugins/request-context.ts.

# Global limit: requests per window per caller.
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=300
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000

# Stricter limit for routes that cost real CPU (equity, coach). Applied per
# route via `app.expensiveRateLimit`.
RATE_LIMIT_EXPENSIVE_MAX=20
RATE_LIMIT_EXPENSIVE_WINDOW_MS=60000

# Guest-session creation, per window, keyed on the caller's ADDRESS rather than
# their session — a route that issues a session cannot be limited by that session,
# because the caller presents a new one on every subsequent request. See the long
# note above `clientKeyedRateLimit` in plugins/security.ts.
#
# This is the limit an automated caller actually hits. RATE_LIMIT_MAX raises only
# the global bucket and has no effect here, so CI and staging set this one
# explicitly. The shipped default is unchanged from when it was a literal.
RATE_LIMIT_GUEST_MAX=10
RATE_LIMIT_GUEST_WINDOW_MS=60000

# OIDC start and callback, address-keyed for the same reason.
RATE_LIMIT_OIDC_MAX=15
RATE_LIMIT_OIDC_WINDOW_MS=60000

# How far past the limit a caller may go before being blocked outright rather
# than merely told to wait.
#
# 0 does NOT disable the block, and this comment used to say it did. @fastify/rate-limit
# computes `isBanned = ban !== -1 && current - max > ban` and treats only a NEGATIVE value as
# "off" — so 0 is the most aggressive setting there is: every single request past the limit is
# a ban. config.ts bounds this variable at `min: 0`, so -1 is refused at boot and the ban
# cannot be switched off from here at all.
#
# An operator who set this to 0 to turn banning off would instead have turned every refused
# request into a `warn` log, a `kind="banned"` metric increment and an audit-log INSERT.
RATE_LIMIT_BAN_AFTER=200

# Unlocks POST /api/v1/_test/rate-limit/reset, which empties every rate-limit
# counter in the process. It exists so `pnpm run test:smoke --strict` can run twice
# in a row: the smoke test proves the guest limiter works by exhausting it, and the
# bucket it drains is address-keyed, so it is the same bucket the next run needs.
#
# SECURITY: whoever holds this token can reset between bursts, which is the same
# thing as having no rate limit at all. A production process that sets this
# variable REFUSES TO START — there is no override, on purpose, because the
# realistic failure is a .env copied from staging with one line nobody re-read.
# Leave it unset everywhere except a developer machine or a CI job.
# RATE_LIMIT_RESET_TOKEN=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Bearer token required to scrape GET /metrics (Prometheus text format).
#
# The scrape body names every route pattern, counts every error code, and reports
# uptime and live connection counts — a description of both the deployment's shape
# and its current load. So it is authenticated.
#
# In production, leaving this unset means the route is NOT REGISTERED at all: a 403
# would confirm both that this software is running here and that somebody meant to
# expose metrics, while a 404 is what every other absent path returns. The trade is
# that an unset token in production means no metrics during an incident, and the
# server says so at boot.
#
# Outside production the endpoint is served without a token, so a developer's first
# encounter with a metric they just added is not a 404.
# METRICS_TOKEN=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Request handling limits
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Largest accepted request body, in bytes. The biggest legitimate body in this
# API is a Monte Carlo request with five ranges, so 64 KiB is generous.
BODY_LIMIT_BYTES=65536

# Milliseconds a single request may take before the socket is closed.
REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# Milliseconds a shutdown waits for in-flight requests before exiting hard.
SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS=10000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Proxy trust  (READ THE WARNING)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# When true, X-Forwarded-For is believed and becomes the client address used for
# rate-limit buckets and the hashed client key in the logs.
#
# SECURITY: if this process can be reached without going through a proxy that
# OVERWRITES that header, any caller can forge their own address — which defeats
# address-based rate limiting entirely and poisons the audit trail. Leave it
# false unless you know the proxy is the only path in.
#
# THE OTHER HALF, which is easy to miss because the safe-looking value causes it:
# behind a reverse proxy with this set to false, `request.ip` is the PROXY's address
# for every browser in the world, so every caller shares one bucket. The routes that
# key on the address rather than the session — guest creation and OIDC — then limit
# the whole deployment at once: ten guest sign-ups per minute in total, exhausted by
# one script, for everybody. That is a self-inflicted outage that looks in the logs
# like the limiter working correctly.
#
# So: false when this process is directly reachable, true when it is genuinely only
# reachable through a proxy that overwrites X-Forwarded-For. The server warns at boot
# when it is false while HOST binds every interface, because that combination is
# almost always the second case misconfigured as the first.
TRUST_PROXY=false

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web client  (apps/web — NOT read by the API server)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE DEPLOYING A BUILT IMAGE. This is the one setting that decides
# where the browser's API requests go, and it is only partly a runtime setting.

# Where the web app's `/api/*` rewrite is proxied to.
#
# Read by apps/web/next.config.ts, by `pnpm run test:smoke` (scripts/smoke-test.mjs)
# and by `pnpm run test:a11y` (scripts/a11y-audit.mjs). Deliberately NOT prefixed
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_`: only the Next server and those scripts read it, never the browser.
#
# There are two topologies and this variable behaves differently in each:
#
#   `pnpm run dev`      web on :3000, api on :3001, nothing in front. `next dev`
#                       reads next.config.ts at start-up, so this IS a runtime
#                       setting here. Change it, restart `pnpm run dev`.
#
#   a built image       `next build` bakes the resolved rewrite destination into
#                       .next/routes-manifest.json and `next start` reads the
#                       manifest, so setting this in the container environment has
#                       NO EFFECT. Retargeting a built image needs the reverse proxy
#                       in front of it (docker-compose.yml, docs/RUNBOOK.md §11) —
#                       that proxy is the single published origin and routes /api/*
#                       and /ws to the API, so the request never reaches the
#                       rewrite. To bake a different default, pass it at BUILD time.
#
# The default is a working `docker run` on a laptop whose API is on :3001, and
# nothing more. Inside the compose network the API is reachable as http://api:3001.
#PRL_API_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001

# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN — deliberately absent, do not reintroduce it.
#
# It named the absolute API origin the browser called directly. Two problems, and
# the second is the deployment blocker:
#
#   1. Two published origins means the session cookie cannot be `__Host-` prefixed
#      with SameSite=Strict, which is what the API's cookie design assumes
#      (apps/api/src/plugins/security.ts).
#   2. `next build` INLINES every `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` value into the client JavaScript.
#      A built image therefore ships whichever origin the build machine saw —
#      http://localhost:3001 by default — and no amount of container environment
#      can change it. Verified: `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3101
#      next build` leaves that literal string in .next/static/chunks/*.js.
#
# The replacement is same-origin relative paths plus the `/api/*` rewrite above.
# If a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable is ever genuinely needed, it must be passed as a
# Docker build arg and documented as build-time-only, here and in the runbook.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Responsible use: the age gate  (apps/web, read at RUNTIME on the server)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# None of these is NEXT_PUBLIC_. They are read by the middleware and by server
# components on each request, so a built image can be pointed at a different
# jurisdiction without rebuilding — which is the whole reason NEXT_PUBLIC_ is
# banned in this repository (see the web section below).
#
# The gate is a SELF-DECLARATION, not verification, and the interstitial says so
# in as many words. It exists because a deployer may need one, not because it
# proves anything about who is reading.

# "on" or "off". Unset means nobody has decided, which is treated as off.
# SHIPPED OFF. Three reasons, and the third is the one people trip on:
#   1. the threshold that is correct depends on a jurisdiction this repository
#      cannot know;
#   2. this is play money with no cash-out, so the gate is a deployer's policy
#      choice rather than a product requirement;
#   3. with it ON, the browser harnesses need PRL_AGE_GATE=off (or a seeded
#      declaration cookie) in their environment, or `pnpm run test:a11y` will
#      audit the interstitial instead of the page and still report PASS.
PRL_AGE_GATE=off

# The declared minimum age. Whole number, clamped to a sane range; anything
# unparseable falls back to 18 and says so at boot.
PRL_AGE_GATE_MINIMUM_AGE=18

# "play" gates the table and the surfaces that lead to it; "site" gates
# everything except the legal pages and the interstitial itself. An unrecognised
# value is treated as "site" — the safer direction — and warns.
PRL_AGE_GATE_SCOPE=play

# Free text naming the jurisdiction whose rule the threshold above is claimed to
# satisfy. Shown to the reader. This repository asserts nothing about whether the
# claim is correct; see docs/production/legal-review-requirements.md.
# PRL_AGE_GATE_JURISDICTION=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Legal pages  (apps/web, read at RUNTIME on the server)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every one of these is null when unset, and the pages render an explicit
# "not configured" marker rather than inventing a value. That is deliberate: a
# blank operator name is visibly missing, while a plausible placeholder reads as
# a reviewed term. Clauses that genuinely need a lawyer are flagged on the page
# AND listed in docs/production/legal-review-requirements.md.

# PRL_LEGAL_OPERATOR=
# PRL_LEGAL_CONTACT=
# PRL_LEGAL_GOVERNING_LAW=
# PRL_LEGAL_HOSTING_REGION=
# YYYY-MM-DD. A date that matches the shape but is not a real date is ignored.
# PRL_LEGAL_EFFECTIVE_DATE=
# One sentence naming a responsible-play organisation and how to reach it.
# PRL_LEGAL_SUPPORT_RESOURCE=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI Legends naming  (packages/poker-ai)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `archetype` (the default, and what production must keep) describes each
# opponent by how it plays. `attributed` puts a real person's name on it.
#
# DO NOT SET THIS TO `attributed` WITHOUT WRITTEN LEGAL REVIEW of name and
# likeness. The profiles are probabilistic policies, not simulations of anybody:
# nothing is claimed to be endorsed and no private thought is ever fabricated.
# The generic-profile fallback exists so a deployment can ship with no names at
# all. See docs/AI-LEGENDS.md and docs/adr/0011.
# PRL_LEGEND_NAMING=archetype

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups  (scripts/backup.mjs, deploy/backup/)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where archives are written. Deliberately OUTSIDE the repository, so an
# encrypted copy of every learner's account is never one `git add -A` from a
# remote. A rehearsal that points this at ./backups should also check .gitignore.
PRL_BACKUP_DIR=/var/backups/poker-risk-lab

# 32 bytes, base64url. Generate with `node scripts/backup.mjs keygen`.
#
# BE HONEST ABOUT WHAT THIS PROTECTS. It encrypts the ARCHIVES, so a stolen
# backup file is useless without it. It does NOT protect the host: anything that
# can read this variable can also read DATABASE_URL and query the live database
# directly. See docs/production/backup-restore.md section 3.
# PRL_BACKUP_KEY=

# Developer machines with no local `pg_dump` can run it inside the compose
# container instead. NOT for production: it needs Docker-socket access, which is
# a far larger grant than taking a backup.
# PRL_BACKUP_PG_CONTAINER=poker-risk-lab-postgres-1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Verification harnesses  (scripts/, never read by the server)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Listed here so every variable in the repository has one place to look it up.

# Which Postgres container the reliability drills create scratch databases in.
# The drills never touch the application database destructively.
# PRL_DRILL_CONTAINER=poker-risk-lab-postgres-1

# Points the smoke harness at the server's RATE_LIMIT_RESET_TOKEN so it can clear
# the counters it is about to spend. Separate from the server's own variable on
# purpose: the harness is a client and must not be configured by the same name as
# the thing it calls. With no token the harness waits out the window instead,
# which is slower and works against any deployment.
# PRL_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_TOKEN=

# Upper bound, in seconds, on that wait before the harness gives up and fails
# honestly rather than hanging.
# PRL_SMOKE_MAX_WAIT_SECONDS=90

# Origins the harnesses talk to. Both default to the development stack.
# PRL_API_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:3001
# PRL_WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Staging stack  (docker-compose.staging.yml, scripts/staging.mjs)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These live in deploy/staging/.env.staging, which is gitignored, NOT in this
# file and NOT in the repository. They are named here so the set is discoverable;
# `pnpm run staging init` generates the secrets and writes that file.
#
#   PRL_STAGING_PUBLIC_ORIGIN            the browser-facing https origin
#   PRL_STAGING_HTTPS_PORT               proxy port
#   PRL_STAGING_DB_PORT / _DB_NAME       staging Postgres
#   PRL_STAGING_DB_SUPERUSER(_PASSWORD)  owner used for migrations
#   PRL_STAGING_API_DB_USER(_PASSWORD)   least-privilege runtime login
#   PRL_STAGING_SESSION_COOKIE_SECRET    32+ bytes, generated
#   PRL_STAGING_METRICS_TOKEN            32+ bytes, generated
#   PRL_STAGING_LOG_LEVEL                defaults to info
#   PRL_API_IMAGE / PRL_WEB_IMAGE        image DIGESTS from the release manifest
#
# Staging runs NODE_ENV=production, so RATE_LIMIT_RESET_TOKEN cannot be set there
# — the API refuses to start with it. That is intended; the smoke harness falls
# back to waiting out the rate-limit window.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by the migration runner only  (apps/api/scripts/migrate.mjs)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not read by the server. Listed here so every variable in the repo has one
# place to look it up.

# Milliseconds to wait for the migration advisory lock before giving up.
# Default 30000.
#PRL_MIGRATE_LOCK_WAIT_MS=30000

# Any non-empty value makes the runner print full stack traces on failure.
#PRL_DEBUG=1

# `seed` and `reset` refuse to run at all when NODE_ENV=production.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compose topology  (docker-compose.yml only — the API server reads none of these)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The composed stack publishes ONE origin: a reverse proxy. The browser talks only
# to it, sends relative `/api/*` paths, and never learns the API's address. Two
# published origins would let a reader land on the one where relative `/api/*` goes
# nowhere, so `web` is deliberately not published.
#
# Where the proxy forwards. Applied when the container is REPLACED, never at build
# time — this is what makes the deployment retargetable. Proven by pointing
# PRL_PROXY_API_UPSTREAM at an unreachable host, observing /api/* 502 while / stays
# 200, and pointing it back: the image ID does not change.
#PRL_PROXY_API_UPSTREAM=api:3001
#PRL_PROXY_WEB_UPSTREAM=web:3000

# Published ports of the proxy and the API. Change these when something else already
# holds the defaults; nothing in the app depends on the numbers.
#WEB_PORT=3000
#API_PORT=3001

# BUILD-TIME ONLY. `next build` bakes the rewrite destination into
# .next/routes-manifest.json and `next start` reads the manifest without re-running
# next.config.ts — so setting this in a running container's environment does NOTHING.
# It is a Docker build arg for local `docker run` and the `next dev` loop only; the
# reverse proxy is the mechanism for a real deployment. The web container asserts this
# at startup and refuses to boot if a build-time-only variable is set in its
# environment, because a silent no-op here is exactly how the previous
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN defect stayed hidden.
#PRL_API_REWRITE_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001

# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN — DELIBERATELY ABSENT. DO NOT REINTRODUCE IT.
# Any NEXT_PUBLIC_* value is inlined into the client bundle at build time, so a built
# image can never be pointed at its own API. It was removed for that reason.
